      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let x be the integer we are looking for. We can set up the inequality x^2 < 2x.\nRearranging the inequality, we have x^2 - 2x < 0.\nFactoring out x, we have x(x - 2) < 0.\nThe product of two numbers is negative if and only if one of them is positive and the other is negative.\nSo, we have two cases to consider:\nCase 1: x > 0 and x - 2 < 0\nIn this case, we have x > 0 and x < 2.\nTherefore, x must be between 0 and 2 (not inclusive).\nCase 2: x < 0 and x - 2 > 0\nIn this case, we have x < 0 and x > 2.\nTherefore, there is no integer that satisfies this case.\nSo, the only integer that satisfies the inequality x^2 < 2x is the one that is between 0 and 2 (not inclusive). The only integer that satisfies this condition is x = 1.\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is $\\boxed{1}$.",
