    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the only integer whose square is less than its double, we need to solve the inequality \\(x^2 < 2x\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the inequality:\n\n\\[x^2 < 2x\\]\n\nNext, we can move all terms to one side of the inequality:\n\n\\[x^2 - 2x < 0\\]\n\nNow, we can factor out an \\(x\\) from the left side:\n\n\\[x(x - 2) < 0\\]\n\nThis inequality holds true when the product of \\(x\\) and \\(x - 2\\) is negative. To determine the intervals where this product is negative, we need to find the roots of the equation \\(x(x - 2) = 0\\). The roots are \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\).\n\nThe roots divide the number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, 0)\\), \\((0, 2)\\), and \\((2, \\infty)\\). We need to test each interval to see where the product \\(x(x - 2)\\) is negative.\n\n1. For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, 0)\\), choose \\(x = -1\\):\n   \\[(-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 > 0\\]\n   The product is positive.\n\n2. For \\(x \\in (0, 2)\\), choose \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[(1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 < 0\\]\n   The product is negative.\n\n3. For \\(x \\in (2, \\infty)\\), choose \\(x = 3\\):\n   \\[(3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 > 0\\]\n   The product is positive.\n\nTherefore, the only interval where the product \\(x(x - 2)\\) is negative is \\((0, 2)\\). This means the only integer \\(x\\) that satisfies the inequality \\(x(x - 2) < 0\\) is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nThus, the only integer whose square is less than its double is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{1}\\]",
