      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $x$ be the integer we are looking for. We want to find the only integer whose square is less than its double, so we can set up the following inequality:\n$x^2 < 2x$\nTo solve this inequality, we can subtract $x^2$ from both sides:\n$0 < 2x - x^2$\nWe can factor out an $x$ from the right side:\n$0 < x(2 - x)$\nSince we are looking for an integer solution, we can set up a table to test different values of $x$:\n$x$ | $2 - x$ | $x(2 - x)$\n1 | 1 | 1\n2 | 0 | 0\n3 | -1 | -3\n4 | -2 | -8\n5 | -3 | -15\n6 | -4 | -24\nFrom the table, we can see that the only integer solution is $x = 1$, since $1(2 - 1) = 1$ is positive.\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
